
2001 Audit Issue Resolution	
Report Name	Findings	Action Steps	Target Dates	Responsible Party	Monitoring Comments	Resolution	
Power Trading Audit Observations	Short power and gas positions of 1.6 MMwh and 2.1 Bcf, respectively, were executed during first and second quarter 2001.  These transactions did not qualify for hedge accounting under SFAS 133, but were not properly marked to market during the appropriate accounting period, resulting in a cumulative misstatement to earnings of $42 million.  In addition, Financial Operations personnel was not aware of these omitted transactions nor were daily P&L reports being prepared for senior management.	The following minimum steps should be taken to ensure that non mark to market transactions are accounted for properly:       · Immediate communication with Financial Operations and RAC personnel via adequate deal documentation (deal ticket, deal “write-up”, etc.);                                        · Documentation of basis for accrual accounting (i.e. hedge accounting, affiliate position, etc.);                                                     · Daily/Weekly/Monthly reporting of P&L to senior management; and                             · Maintenance of accrual positions within RiskTrac.	8/31/2001	Stacey White	
C	As a result of the anomalies in the West market, management has not consistently accounted for transmission contracts.  Currently, management accounts for these contracts on an accrual basis	Transmission contracts should be accounted for at fair value  in accordance with EITF 98-10 since they qualify as energy-trading contracts.  If anomalies exist that cause management to question valuation, justifiable adjustments may be made, but must also be documented.  	12/31/2001	Stacey White       John Postlethwaite	
D	Due to the absence of a correlation matrix as well as the addition to and changes in curves traded in the East,  transmission deals are currently not accounted for under mark-to-market accounting. 	Transmission contracts should be accounted for at fair value in accordance with EITF 98-10 since they qualify as energy-trading contracts.  Management should consider putting in place a correlation matrix to properly value these deals.  	12/31/2001	Stacey White       John Postlethwaite	
E	Management is currently involved in TCC contracts and previously FTR contracts.  These contracts  should normally be accounted for under mark-to-market accounting, however as there is no liquid market for them, they are unable to be marked.  Although, the risk exposure is rigorously monitored by reviewing congestion patterns and other daily market information, these contracts expose the company to risk that can not be monitored within the standard, preestablished risk and VAR limits	All levels of management should be aware of the risks created from these contracts.  In addition, management should determine how these risks should be appropriately captured and reported	9/30/2001	Stacey White       	